+#### NIF crate isolation
+
+Enable `nif_crate_isolation` when independent NIF crates share one Rustler
+instance in a linked library. The feature separates their function tables and
+resource registrations so each init entry registers the NIFs belonging to its
+Erlang module. Use one `rustler::init!` entry per NIF crate.
+
+The aggregate crate enables the feature on its Rustler dependency:
+
+```toml
+[dependencies]
+rustler = { version = "0.39.0", default-features = false, features = ["nif_crate_isolation"] }
+```
+
+Cargo unifies the feature across dependencies using the same Rustler package.
+Registrations identify their originating crate name and package version.
+For registrations defined in helper crates, the aggregate links typed ownership
+records with `rustler::nif_crate_owners!`. The optional host tool
+[`rustler-bundler`](rustler_bundler/README.md) derives those records from Cargo's
+resolved dependency graph and generates the aggregate crate. Its guide covers
+Rebar3/Mix integration, manual aggregate creation and the required final linking
+and static NIF loader setup. The runtime feature is usable without the tool.
+
+The feature synchronizes the shared resource registry during registration and
+lookup. Ownership metadata and registry locking are compiled only when the
+feature is enabled. Each Rust resource type has one owning Erlang module;
+a conflicting registration fails without replacing the original handle.
+Distinct resource types can use the same Erlang resource name. Aggregates
+require compatible Rustler versions and unambiguous crate identities.
+
 #### Minimal Supported Rust Version (MSRV)
 
 Rustler currently has a minimal supported Rust version (MSRV) of 1.91. This
