+++ b/rustler_bundler/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,197 @@
+# Rustler bundler
+
+`rustler-bundler` prepares a single Rust `staticlib` containing independent
+Rustler NIF crates. Build systems can invoke it with a JSON specification and
+consume its JSON output.
+
+## Installation
+
+From the Rustler source tree:
+
+```sh
+cargo install --path rustler_bundler
+rustler-bundler --version
+```
+
+## Preparing and building a bundle
+
+Each NIF crate uses ordinary Rustler attributes and one `rustler::init!`.
+It must provide `lib` or `rlib` output so Cargo can link it as a dependency:
+
+```toml
+[lib]
+crate-type = ["cdylib", "rlib"]
+```
+
+Create a specification, for example `/project/bundle.json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "schema": 1,
+  "out_dir": "/project/_build/nif-bundle",
+  "inputs": [
+    {
+      "id": "math",
+      "manifest_path": "/project/native/math/Cargo.toml",
+      "features": [],
+      "default_features": true
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "image",
+      "manifest_path": "/project/deps/image/native/image/Cargo.toml"
+    }
+  ],
+  "cargo": ["cargo"],
+  "release": true,
+  "target_dir": "/project/_build/rust-target"
+}
+```
+
+Each input identifies a package or workspace manifest. The tool selects its
+workspace default members and library targets producing `cdylib` or `staticlib`;
+`library_types` can override those output kinds. Features and `default_features`
+apply to the selected packages. Each input's unique `id` is echoed in its
+`entries[].input` values, allowing callers to associate entries with their inputs.
+Paths must be absolute; `out_dir` is owned by the generator. Features are
+unqualified Cargo feature names.
+
+Callers that already know their library targets can provide `roots` instead of
+`inputs`. Each root specifies `manifest_path`, `package` (Cargo package name),
+`library` (library target name), and optional `features` and `default_features`.
+
+The optional `cargo` command array may select a toolchain (`["cargo", "+nightly"]`)
+or an explicit Cargo executable.
+`target` selects a target triple or JSON target specification. All Cargo
+invocations inherit the caller's environment, including linker configuration
+and `CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS`.
+
+The optional `offline` boolean defaults to false. When true, the tool passes
+`--offline` to Cargo for dependency resolution and compilation, preventing
+network access. Required dependencies must already be available locally.
+For a separate Cargo invocation after `prepare`, pass `--offline` to that
+command too if needed.
+
+```sh
+rustler-bundler prepare --spec /project/bundle.json
+cargo build --manifest-path /project/_build/nif-bundle/Cargo.toml \
+    --release --target-dir /project/_build/rust-target
+```
+
+Alternatively, the tool can perform both steps using the specification's
+`cargo`, `release`, `target`, `target_dir` and `offline` settings:
+
+```sh
+rustler-bundler build --spec /project/bundle.json
+```
+
+`prepare` writes one JSON result to stdout, with `schema: 1`,
+`reason: "rustler-bundle"`, `manifest_path`, `package`, `library`,
+`ownership_path`, `ownership`, `archive` and `entries`. Each entry contains its
+library name, named initialization symbol (such as `math_nif_nif_init`), and
+input ID when discovered from `inputs`. `archive` is null after preparation.
+`build` forwards Cargo's JSON messages, including compiler diagnostics, and sets
+`archive` to the produced file's path. An input selection containing no matching
+libraries returns no entries and a null archive. Progress and tool errors go to
+stderr; a nonzero exit status means the operation failed.
+
+The generated crate depends on every root and enables `nif_crate_isolation`
+on the same resolved Rustler package. Cargo configuration inherited from the
+output directory's ancestors supplies patches, profiles and source overrides.
+For a separate output tree, provide equivalent Cargo configuration there.
+All roots must use one compatible Rustler package; incompatible sources or
+versions are rejected.
+
+The tool walks normal library dependencies in the target-filtered Cargo graph,
+stopping at other NIF roots. It generates typed ownership records in
+`src/ownership.rs`; dependency source code needs no ownership annotations or
+environment variables. Proc macros and build/dev dependencies are excluded.
+
+## Linking into a runtime
+
+The archive contains all roots and a single Rust dependency graph. Link it
+**once**, retaining its inventory constructors and ownership records. On GNU
+linkers this generally means:
+
+```sh
+cc ... -Wl,--whole-archive /path/to/librustler_nif_bundle.a \
+    -Wl,--no-whole-archive ...
+```
+
+Use the corresponding archive-retention mechanism on other linkers. Preserve
+constructor sections through the final executable link and linker script.
+Native system libraries depend on the Rust target and NIF dependencies; inspect
+`cargo rustc -- --print=native-static-libs` for the final bundle's requirements.
+Do not combine independently built Rust staticlibs or change to `panic=abort`.
+
+Register **every** `entries[].init` symbol with the runtime's static NIF loader,
+mapping it to the filename passed to `erlang:load_nif/2`. The Erlang/Elixir module
+name remains the name supplied to `rustler::init!`; it is different from this
+library/entry name. OTP must support multiple named entries from one archive,
+or its build integration must supply the equivalent static lookup table. Adding
+the archive alone is insufficient. Platform integrations own that final link,
+table generation and runtime packaging.
+
+Rebar3 integrations can supply input manifests and feature selections, invoke
+`build`, and consume its archive path and entries. Cargo compiler diagnostics
+remain available in the JSON stream. Disable separate
+per-crate compilation for the static target, retain its loader/header setup,
+and retain ordinary dynamic compilation for host builds.
+
+Mix integrations can invoke the executable once for an application or release
+and configure each `use Rustler` module with `skip_compilation?: true` for the
+static target. Preserve each module's loading filename and load data and add
+matching entries to OTP's static loader. The existing Mix compiler copies
+dynamic libraries; it does not itself build/link this static bundle. No new Mix
+task is included here. Ordinary Mix dynamic builds need no configuration change.
+
+## Workspace policy
+
+Set the optional `workspace_manifest` to the owning application's Cargo.toml.
+The bundle inherits that workspace's resolver, profiles, `[patch]`, `[replace]`
+and Cargo feature gates. Relative patch paths are resolved from the original
+workspace root. Dependency workspaces supply ordinary libraries; their profiles
+and patch tables do not override the application's settings.
+
+The owning workspace's Cargo.lock seeds the derived lockfile. Its locked
+registry versions and Git revisions are preserved for its reachable dependencies.
+The bundler attempts to reconcile the combined graph with those choices and
+reports a conflict when the requirements cannot preserve them. Update the
+application's dependency requirements and lockfile deliberately to resolve such
+a conflict. New dependencies without an existing locked choice resolve normally.
+Dependency library lockfiles do not control resolution, matching normal Cargo
+behavior. Without an owning lockfile, Cargo maintains the generated lockfile. Explicit patches, including SDK patches, can replace registry
+packages with local sources.
+
+The bundler writes only its output directory, leaving input manifests and locks
+unchanged. `build` uses `--locked` after resolution. Results include
+`workspace_inputs`: the source workspace manifest and lock paths, including
+missing lock paths, for build systems to track creation, changes and deletion.
+The normal Cargo configuration search still starts in the generated bundle;
+put shared `.cargo/config.toml` settings in an ancestor of that directory.
+
+## Manual aggregate creation
+
+The runtime feature can also be used without this executable. Create a Cargo
+`staticlib` depending on all NIF roots and the same Rustler package with
+`default-features = false, features = ["nif_crate_isolation"]`. Retain each root
+with `extern crate root_alias;` and declare helper ownership in the aggregate:
+
+```rust
+extern crate math_nif;
+extern crate image_nif;
+rustler::nif_crate_owners! {
+    "math_helper@0.1.0" => "math_nif@0.1.0",
+    "image_helper@0.2.0" => "image_nif@0.1.0",
+}
+```
+
+Keys use Rust library crate names (hyphens become underscores) and package
+versions. Include all transitive helper libraries containing registrations;
+stop traversal at another independently initialized NIF root. A helper used by
+two roots can have two records. A given Rust resource type can only belong to
+one Erlang module: loading a second owner fails without replacing its handle.
+Distinct Rust types may use the same Erlang resource name. Same-name/version
+crates from different sources cannot be distinguished and are rejected by the
+tool. All NIF roots must be known to the bundle; arbitrary precompiled archives
+cannot be merged into this shared Rust graph. These restrictions also apply to
+manually assembled bundles.
